{ Now we'll look at the Fishing Pole Trap from the Berlin Defense. }
1. e4 { Play e5! } { [%csl Ge5][%cal Ge7e5] } 1... e5 2. Nf3 { Play Nc6! } { [%csl Gc6][%cal Gb8c6] } 2... Nc6 3. Bb5 { Now, Nf6 is the Berlin Defense! } { [%csl Gf6][%cal Gg8f6] } 3... Nf6 4. O-O { White castles. Now we play Ng4! } { [%csl Gg4][%cal Gf6g4] } 4... Ng4?! { So, Ng4 is a dubious move and the Fishing Pole Trap is less effective in the Berlin Defense than the Exchange Variation, because the Knight, unlike the Bishop, is of no use on g4. But this trap is worth a try! } 5. h3 { White plays h3, as always. Play h5 } { [%csl Gh5][%cal Gh7h5] } 5... h5 { Now, the best move is c3, but if they take- } 6. hxg4?? { The same things here too! } { [%csl Gg4][%cal Gh5g4] } 6... hxg4 { And c3 is the best move, we take the Knight and it's winning. But, if white tries to save the knight again- } 7. Nh2 { Play Qh4! } { [%csl Gh4][%cal Gd8h4] } 7... Qh4 8. f4 { We just lock the King there. } { [%csl Gg3,Bf2][%cal Gg4g3,Rg1f2,Bg3f2] } 8... g3 { And we're winning! But the Fishing Pole Trap is more effective in the Exchange Variation. } *

