{ The next trap is the Elephant Trap, also called the Cambridge Springs Trap. This trap also occurs in the Queen's Gambit Declined Variation. }
1. d4 { Play d5! } { [%csl Gd5][%cal Gd7d5] } 1... d5 2. c4 { This is the Queen's Gambit. Play e6! } { [%csl Ge6][%cal Ge7e6] } 2... e6 { Queen's Gambit Declined! } 3. Nc3 { Play Nf6! } { [%csl Gf6][%cal Gg8f6] } 3... Nf6 4. Bg5 { White goes for one of the most common moves, Bg5. And now this is where the trap begins. Normally, most people just play Be7 and play the normal theory. BUT, there is another move that we can play! Can you guess it? } 4... Nbd7! { Yes! We play Nbd7! } 5. cxd5 { We take the pawn. } 5... exd5 6. Nxd5?? { Oh, no! Our pawn is lost, or is it? } 6... Nxd5!! { NO! It's not! We take the Knight! SACRIFICING THE QUEEN!!!!!!!!!!!!!!!!!!!!!!!!!!!!!!!!!!!!!!!!!!!!!!!!!!!!!!!!!!!!!!!!!!!!!! } 7. Bxd8?? { And if White takes, IT'S AN EVEN BIGGER BLUNDER! Why? } 7... Bb4+ { Yes! We give a check! Remember that the Elephant Trap doesn't work if White's Knight is on f3, because they could just block with the Knight and we're losing. BUT, right now, White has to block with the queen! } { [%csl Re1][%cal Rb4e1] } 8. Qd2 { We just take the queen! } 8... Bxd2+ 9. Kxd2 { Now the Bishop! } { [%csl Gd8][%cal Ge8d8] } 9... Kxd8 { And finally, after all the dust is settled, we can see that Black is a clear piece up! } *

