{ The sixth trap is called the Lasker Trap or Albin Countergambit Trap, named after the second world chess champion, Emanuel Lasker. }
1. d4 { It arises from the Queen's Gambit. Play d5. } { [%csl Gd5][%cal Gd7d5] } 1... d5 2. c4 { And now, the normal moves are e6, c6, dxc4, or Nf6. BUT, we're not going to play any of them! We will play e5! } { [%csl Ge5][%cal Ge7e5] } 2... e5 { And this is the aggressive, Albin Countergambit! If white plays cxd5, then we just recapture it and the position is fine. But, most of the time, White plays- } 3. dxe5 { dxe5! Now we push our pawn to d4! } { [%csl Gd4][%cal Gd5d4] } 3... d4 4. e3 { Most of the time, White will play e3 to try and exchange the d4 pawn. But, we don't take the e3 pawn. Instead, we play Bb4+! } { [%csl Gb4][%cal Gf8b4] } 4... Bb4+ 5. Bd2 { White defends the King. And now, we play dxe3! } { [%csl Ge3][%cal Gd4e3] } 5... dxe3 { Black sacrifices THE BISHOP!!!!!!!!!!!!!!!!!!!!!!!!!!!!!!!!!!!! The best move is fxe3, followed by Qh4+ and g3. BUT, if white gets greedy and takes our Bishop- } { [%csl Rb4] } 6. Bxb4 { BOOM! White has fallen into our trap! We take the pawn! } { [%csl Gf2][%cal Ge3f2] } 6... exf2+ { White can't take because of Qxd1! So they play Ke2! } { [%csl Rf2][%cal Re1f2,Bd8d1] } 7. Ke2 { What do we do now??? } 7... fxg1=N+!! { Yes! We underpromote to a KNIGHT! This is because it also gives White a check! Now, the best move is Ke1, followed by - Qh4+ , Kd2 and Nc6. BUT, if white takes- } { [%csl Re2][%cal Rg1e2] } (7... fxg1=Q?? { Promoting to a queen is a BLUNDER because white can take our d8 queen with a check! And then take the second queen afterwards, and the position will be equalized again! } { [%cal Rd1d8,Be8d8,Rh1g1] }) 8. Rxg1?? { It's another blunder! Why? } 8... Bg4+ { A skewer! And Black wins the queen! Black wins! } { [%csl Re2][%cal Rg4d1] } *

